### Coverage

{{if .Coverage.Available -}}
These numbers come from `dummyrun coverage` over the same runs. They say how much the sample can tell you.

- Counted: {{.Coverage.Basis}}
- Completed runs: {{.Coverage.CompletedRuns}}
- Distinct problems seen: {{.Coverage.Observed}}
- Still unfound: {{if .Coverage.UnfoundAtLeast}}at least {{f1 .Coverage.UnfoundAtLeast}} (Chao2 lower bound of {{f1 .Coverage.TotalAtLeast}} in total, {{.Coverage.Form}} form){{else}}not estimable from these runs{{end}}
- Sample coverage: {{if .Coverage.SampleCoverage}}{{f2 .Coverage.SampleCoverage}}{{else}}not estimable{{end}}
- New problems per extra run: {{if .Coverage.NewPerExtraRun}}{{f2 .Coverage.NewPerExtraRun}}{{else}}not estimable{{end}}
{{- if .Coverage.RouteUniverse}}
- Routes visited: {{.Coverage.RoutesVisited}} of {{.Coverage.RouteUniverse}} known routes
{{- end}}
- Stopping rule: {{.Coverage.StoppingRule}}

How to read these numbers:

- The Chao2 figure is a floor. The real number of problems is at least that high. It is never a count of what remains.
- Sample coverage is the share of the chance of finding a problem that sits in problems already found. Below 0.50 do not extrapolate from it.
- New problems per extra run is how many new problems one more run is expected to find. A low figure suggests more runs will add little.
- With fewer than 5 completed runs the estimates are weak. The stopping rule needs 4 completed runs in each group.
